  "query_preview": "..Fast moving ridge passes through the area today and\ntonight with dry conditions and cool conditions with temperatures\nabout 5 degrees below average.  \n\nThe next storm system arrives late Thursday afternoon. Fairly \nrobust Pacific Storm that's moving onshore in California today \nwill weaken substantially as it approaches our region. With a more\nnortherly origin, this will be a colder system with the potential\nto drop snow levels as low as 6000ft Friday. However the 500mb \nLow associated with this system will stay north in Utah opening up\nas it does so and keeping most of the energy north with it. The \ntrailing cold front will be washing out as it quickly moves across\nthe state so with the exception of the Kaibab Plateau potentially\nseeing a couple inches, the rest of the area will see a quick \ndusting of less than an inch above 6500-7000ft Thursday night \ninto Friday morning before the system moves away Friday afternoon.\nOverall QPF looks very meager with most areas not even seeing a \ntenth of an inch of rain or liquid equivalent. The pressure \ngradient along the front will elevate south to southwesterly winds\n15-30 mph with gusts of 40-50 mph again so Wind Advisories may \nneed to be issued from Thursday afternoon into Friday afternoon. \n\nOnce this system gets out of here Friday evening, the weekend \nlooks dry as weak high pressure builds into the area. Temperatures\nwon't recover much with highs 5 to 10 degrees below average. \nThere is the potential for another storm system to impact the area\non Monday. Models have been pretty poor on handling the timing \nand location of this system so confidence is low right now on what\nwill take place. Will have to keep monitoring to see what will go\non.",
